WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.800
A gram of sweat that evaporates from your skin carries off about 2.4 kilojoules of

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.160
heat. A gram that drips off carries away almost none.

3
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.160
So the cooling comes from evaporation, not from sweat itself.

4
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.040
Skin cools you only as fast as air takes up water vapour.

5
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.940
On a humid day, that can lag behind the heat your muscles make.

6
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:27.023
For a person, heat balance is a sum.

7
00:00:27.503 --> 00:00:32.723
Heat storage equals metabolic rate, minus external work, plus or minus dry exchange,

8
00:00:33.123 --> 00:00:34.383
minus evaporative loss.

9
00:00:35.103 --> 00:00:39.503
Dry exchange means radiant, convective and conductive heat, and it can run either

10
00:00:39.503 --> 00:00:41.543
way. Every term is in watts.

11
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:44.263
When storage is zero, body temperature is steady.

12
00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:49.937
Take illustrative numbers for a healthy 70-kilogram adult cycling steadily in hot,

13
00:00:50.157 --> 00:00:55.817
sunny air. Metabolic rate is 1,000 watts, and 20 % of it becomes external work.

14
00:00:56.257 --> 00:00:58.237
That leaves 800 watts of heat.

15
00:00:58.917 --> 00:01:02.897
Dry heat gain adds 50 watts, and respiratory heat loss is ignored.

16
00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:07.537
To keep storage at zero, evaporation must remove 850 watts.

17
00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:13.517
Evaporating sweat takes up about 2.43 kilojoules per gram at skin temperature.

18
00:01:13.877 --> 00:01:20.477
So 850 watts needs about 1.26 litres of sweat per hour, if every gram evaporates.

19
00:01:21.177 --> 00:01:24.637
Suppose only 70 % evaporates and the rest drips.

20
00:01:25.077 --> 00:01:28.377
The sweat rate then rises to about 1.80 litres per hour.

21
00:01:28.957 --> 00:01:33.057
Without drinking, body mass falls about 2.6 % in that hour.

22
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:41.027
Now let humid air cap evaporation at 700 watts when the body needs 850 The gap of

23
00:01:41.027 --> 00:01:46.207
150 watts is stored as heat, 540 kilojoules in an hour.

24
00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:50.787
Take 2.98 kilojoules per kilogram per degree Celsius.

25
00:01:51.487 --> 00:01:55.527
Then 70 kilogram stores about 209 kilojoules per degree.

26
00:01:55.527 --> 00:02:01.607
If body temperature is uniform, it rises about 2.6 degrees Celsius per hour.

27
00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:07.237
Once the heat that must evaporate exceeds the most that air allows, sweating harder

28
00:02:07.237 --> 00:02:09.257
adds dripping, not cooling.

29
00:02:10.297 --> 00:02:11.757
Balance returns only three ways.

30
00:02:12.297 --> 00:02:14.177
Make less heat by slowing down.

31
00:02:14.777 --> 00:02:16.957
Lose more dry heat with wind or shade.

32
00:02:17.277 --> 00:02:18.657
Or have drier air.

33
00:02:19.277 --> 00:02:23.437
These numbers are made up for practice, and the rate predicts no real person.

34
00:02:24.097 --> 00:02:29.417
Keep this rule. Sweat cools only when it evaporates, and the air sets how much can.
